# TLS chain supplement for sharedsystems.dhsoha.state.or.us
#
# WHAT THIS IS. The intermediate certificate that host's own certificate names, and does not
# send. Supplying it lets a strict client build a path to a root the system already trusts;
# it does NOT relax verification, and it is not a trust anchor -- OpenSSL still requires the
# path to terminate at a self-signed certificate in the system store. See ADR 0007 here and
# corpus-toolkit ADR 0012 for the measurement behind that claim.
#
#   Subject   DigiCert Global G2 TLS RSA SHA256 2020 CA1  (C=US, O=DigiCert Inc)
#   Issuer    DigiCert Global Root G2                     (in every mainstream trust store)
#   Valid     2021-03-30 -> 2031-03-29
#   Retrieved 2026-08-27 from the AIA extension of the leaf served by
#             sharedsystems.dhsoha.state.or.us:443 --
#             http://cacerts.digicert.com/DigiCertGlobalG2TLSRSASHA2562020CA1-1.crt
#
# WHEN TO DELETE THIS FILE. When the server serves its own chain. That is checkable rather
# than a matter of waiting to hear: corpus_toolkit.sources.tls.supplement_still_needed()
# re-requests the host without this file and reports which of the three answers it got.
# `corpus-detect-changes` refuses to load this within 30 days of notAfter, so a spent
# supplement fails by name rather than as a mysterious fetch failure.
